::: {.callout-warning title="Rules of the top-up for pre-registration"}
- **The rule, not the number, is pre-registered.** Look 2 calibrates the Naive
  analysis set to the smallest sample that yields 80% overall power under the
  *Worst* scenario, given &alpha; = .045
  at look 1 and the look-1 sample size reached.
- **Only outcome-blind quantities enter the rule**, i.e., the look-1 sample and
  the retention. The look-1 estimate of blocking is not considered when
  computing look 2 sample size requirements.
- **The critical values are recomputed** for the sample sizes actually reached
  at each look, keeping &alpha; = .045 at
  look 1 and an overall &alpha; = .05.
- **Look 2 is skipped when BI~2~ analysis detects blocking at look 1**, in which
  case replication is declared successful and *d*~z~ is estimated with its 95%
  CI for the ensuing extension study (which, unlike the replication, tests
  against a two-sided alternative hypothesis).
- **The interim analysis at look 1 is the only planned look at BI~2~** before
  look 2. Attrition, completion time and the yield of the learning check may be
  monitored throughout.
- **After look 2, the effect size is reported as the median-unbiased
  estimate** with its confidence interval under the stage-wise ordering. Both
  correct the bias that a naive estimate carries after a sequential design.
- **BI~3~ is tested in parallel at both looks against the same boundaries.**
  At look 1, BI~3~ reaches 94% power if
  its true effect equals the Full sample estimate and
  83% if it equals the Learned set
  estimate.
- **Look 2 is skipped when BI~3~ analysis detects blocking at look 1, even if
  BI~2~ fails to do so.** This is the only rule that stops the study without
  rejecting the null hypothesis for BI~2~, so it cannot raise its false-positive
  rate.
:::

::: {.callout-warning title="Practical caps on the sample size"}
- **A cap on look 2.** If the look 2 rule asks for more participants than the
  LABELS pool can supply in one semester, a cap will be fixed at the number of
  participants available, and the power it achieves will be reported.
- **Look 1 below its minimum.** If fewer than the 72 minimum
  participants reach the Naive set during look-1 data collection, the sampling
  will continue in smaller batches, aiming at recruiting the additional
  participants necessary to reach the 90 planned Naive set
  participants given the retention rate observed until then. This process
  continues until at least 72 participants reach the Naive set.
:::

# Decision tree

Based on the outcome of the two looks at the data specified above, the results
of `fba-allergy-online-02` lead through a single, unambiguous decision tree,
presented in the table and figure below.

Its guiding principles are:

* Each step is decided by a success or failure to reject the null hypothesis of
  no blocking in favour of its directed alternative of blocking greater than
  zero according to the decision thresholds derived by the power analysis.
* BI~2~ analysis results decide the replication success or failure statement.
* BI~3~ analysis results guide blocking index selection for the follow-up
  extension study.
* Replication statement and next study index selection are two conceptually
  independent tasks that proceed in parallel and do not depend on each other
  (i.e., the study may continue to the next experiment with BI~3~ as its index
  even if the replication of the original findings fails).
* All statements are based on the Naive analysis set results, with the Learned
  and Full sets serving only as sensitivity analysis samples and for exploratory
  analyses distinct from the study's primary objective.

::: {.callout-warning title="Replication statement"}
The study was calibrated to be able to detect BI~2~-based blocking in the first
test block, thereby replicating the behavioural findings of @beesley2011.
Whether the replication is successful or not is decided solely on the basis of
testing $\mathbb{E}(BI_2) = 0$ against its $\mathbb{E}(BI_2) > 0$ alternative.
The replication is declared successful as soon as BI~2~ analysis detects
blocking, at look 1 or at look 2, and unsuccessful if BI~2~ analysis fails at
look 1 while BI~3~ detects blocking, or if it fails at look 2.
:::

::: {.callout-tip title="The role of BI~3~"}
The BI~3~ choice-based blocking index analysis is not part of the replication
proper, because @beesley2011 used a single test block with BI~2~ as their sole
measure of blocking. However, the overarching goal of the current study is not
only to replicate but also extend the findings to allow for differentiation
between attentional shift and attentional competition accounts of the effect.
This extension is directly inspired by @paskewitz2020's simulation study, which
uses BI~3~ as its outcome scale. Furthermore, existing evidence about response
scale in blocking experiments indicates that choice-based variables may be more
sensitive to associative processes than rating-based ones, which seem to be
more sensitive to deliberate reasoning [@don2018].

Taken together, unlike the replication study, the `fba-allergy-online-03`
extension experiment benefits from adopting BI~3~ as its primary endpoint
instead of BI~2~. Consequently, selection of the index for the follow-up
extension study is based on testing $\mathbb{E}(BI_3) = 0$ against its
$\mathbb{E}(BI_3) > 0$ alternative.
:::

::: {.callout-important title="What if no blocking is detected"}
If neither index detects blocking at either look, the project changes course.
Importantly, the extension will not be run next, as it would remain unclear
whether the design can detect blocking in the online sample available. Instead,
blocking would be sought in a different setting, e.g., a laboratory replication
with eye tracking to reproduce more closely the setting used by @beesley2011.
:::

