# Conclusions

* **Effect size.** The original study reported *d*~z~ &approx;
  0.54, while the feasibility study suggests a smaller
  effect, *d*~z~ = 0.35 in the Full sample and
  0.37 in the Learned set, roughly halved in the
  Naive set (0.15). These estimates are used to define
  the *Best*, *Realistic* and *Worst* scenarios.
* **Look 1.** The replication recruits 180 participants and analyses
  the Naive set once at least 72 participants reach it, testing BI~2~
  at a one-sided &alpha; = .045. With the
  planned 90 participants, this sample size yields
  95% power under the *Realistic* scenario.
* **Look 2.** If neither index detects blocking at look 1, the Naive set is
  topped up to about 441 participants at the assumed retention of 50%
  (about 882 recruited in total), tested at a nominal
  one-sided *p* < .007, for 80%
  overall power under the *Worst* scenario.
* **The alternative index.** BI~3~ is tested alongside BI~2~ using the same
  thresholds. At look 1, it reaches 94%
  power if its true effect equals the Full sample estimate and
  83% if it equals the Learned set
  estimate.
* **Decisions.** BI~2~ alone decides whether @beesley2011's blocking is
  replicated; BI~3~ decides which index will be used by `fba-allergy-online-03`
  extension study.

::: {.callout-warning title="Implications for the pre-registration"}
The pre-registration of `fba-allergy-online-02` registers the hypotheses
(blocking in BI~2~ and in BI~3~ in the Naive set), the look-1 sampling rule,
the split of &alpha; between the looks, the rule that sets the look-2 sample
from the retention look 1 achieves, and the decision tree. The cap on look 2
remains to be fixed once the size of the LABELS pool is known.
:::

